{ Editorial study excerpt from Aman Hambleton's London series. The video handle was systemplayer; the recovered archive uses systematic. Result * marks an excerpt, not a change to the actual game result. Comments and analysis variations are editorial. https://www.chess.com/game/live/50357446737 Aman Hambleton's London System, Part 1. Verified game/review point at 13:40; this is not an exact game-start marker. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3bULUDwHYtc&list=PLUjxDD7HNNTiljFCufDXDSjEQH1cIdbSx&t=820s } 1. d4 d5 2. Bf4 Bf5 3. c4 dxc4 4. Nc3 Nc6 5. e4 { After 5.e4: ...dxc4 let White take central space while attacking the bishop on f5. Recover c4 and develop. } 5... Bg6 ( 5... Qxd4 6. Qc2 Bd7 7. Nb5 { Editorial analysis: Black can counterattack on d4 instead of immediately saving Bf5. Qc2 keeps play active; e4 does not win material by force. } ) 6. d5 Nb4 7. Qa4+ Nc6 8. dxc6 bxc6 9. Qxc6+ Qd7 10. Qxa8+ { Recorded continuation. See the complete corpus for the rest of the game and its actual result. } *

